{"causes": ["The inherent political tensions between the increasingly powerful Kamakura Shogunate and the traditional authority of the Imperial Court set the stage for conflict.", "Emperor Go-Toba's fervent patronage of waka poetry fostered a vibrant literary environment in which his son, Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō, could develop his artistic talents.", "The Jōkyū War of 1221, initiated by Emperor Go-Toba to restore imperial power, directly led to the exile and political marginalization of the imperial family, including Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō."], "effects": ["Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō's exile to Sado Island solidified the Kamakura Shogunate's dominance over the imperial court, reducing the emperor to a largely ceremonial figure.", "His poetic contributions helped perpetuate the waka tradition during a period of political upheaval, enriching the *Shoku Gosen Wakashū* and other imperial anthologies.", "His life story became a symbol of the imperial family's resilience and cultural enduring power even after a significant loss of political authority."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "go-toba", "sourceName": "Emperor Go-Toba", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "djo-nydshinn", "targetName": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō", "context": "Emperor Go-Toba's fervent patronage of waka poetry and his political ambitions profoundly influenced the cultural development and eventual exile of his son, Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō, in the early 13th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "djo-nydshinn", "sourceName": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "imperial-waka-poetry-circles", "targetName": "Imperial Waka Poetry Circles", "context": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō actively participated in the imperial waka poetry circles in Kyoto, contributing to uta-awase and having his poems selected for court anthologies during the early Kamakura period."}, {"sourceSlug": "djo-nydshinn", "sourceName": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "sado-island-japan", "targetName": "Sado Island, Japan", "context": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō spent the final decades of his life in enforced exile on Sado Island from 1221 until his death in 1249, following the Jōkyū War."}, {"sourceSlug": "jokyu-war", "sourceName": "Jōkyū War", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "djo-nydshinn", "targetName": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō", "context": "The imperial defeat in the Jōkyū War of 1221 directly caused Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō's permanent exile from the capital, alongside other members of the imperial family."}, {"sourceSlug": "djo-nydshinn", "sourceName": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "shoku-gosen-wakashu", "targetName": "Shoku Gosen Wakashū", "context": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō contributed multiple waka poems to the *Shoku Gosen Wakashū*, an imperial anthology compiled around 1251, solidifying his place in Japanese literary history."}], "places": [{"name": "Kyoto, Japan", "role": "Birthplace & Cultural Center"}, {"name": "Sado Island, Japan", "role": "Place of Exile & Death"}, {"name": "Kamakura, Japan", "role": "Seat of Rival Power"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "dōjo-nyūdōshinnō"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3403322", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_D%C5%8Djony%C5%ABd%C5%8D"], "tags": ["Japan", "Religious Figures", "Asia", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō is remembered as a landmark person of the Medieval era, referenced in 0 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō's life and exile symbolize the dramatic shift of political power from the imperial court to the Kamakura Shogunate after the Jōkyū War, while his poetic contributions ensured the continuity of the waka tradition during this turbulent era.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-17T08:08:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27674719244", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Prince Dōjonyūdō was a waka poet and Japanese nobleman active in the early Kamakura period. He was a son of Emperor Go-Toba.", "newValue": "Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō (1196–1249), born Prince Hidenaga, was a cloistered imperial prince and a significant waka poet whose life vividly encapsulated the profound shift in power from the imperial court to the Kamakura Shogunate during Japan's early Medieval period. As a son of the influential Retired Emperor Go-Toba, Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō found himself at the epicenter of political and cultural ferment, his personal trajectory inextricably linked to the imperial family's doomed attempt to reclaim temporal authority in the Jōkyū War of 1221. His contributions to Japanese poetry, alongside his enforced exile, mark him as a poignant figure symbolizing both artistic continuity and the dramatic decline of imperial political sway.

Following his father's abdication, Prince Hidenaga entered the Buddhist priesthood, adopting the name Dōjo (道助), a common path for imperial scions during this era to avoid direct political entanglements while maintaining cultural influence. He was an active participant in the refined imperial poetry circles, contributing numerous poems to prestigious uta-awase (poetry contests) and having his works selected for imperial anthologies such as the *Shoku Gosen Wakashū* (c. 1251). His poetic style often reflected the melancholic beauty and aesthetic sensibilities characteristic of the late Heian and early Kamakura periods, blending classical forms with a nascent awareness of the era's martial realities.

The devastating defeat of the imperial forces in the Jōkyū War led to severe repercussions for Emperor Go-Toba and his close family. Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō, despite his monastic status, was deemed complicit due to his lineage and close association with his father's court. Consequently, he was exiled to Sado Island in Echigo Province, mirroring the banishment of his half-brother, Emperor Juntoku, and his father to Oki Island. He spent the remainder of his life in isolation, dying on Sado Island in 1249, a mere decade before his father's own death in exile."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T08:08:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27674719244", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Educated at in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Influenced by in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Born into in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}], "newValue": ["The inherent political tensions between the increasingly powerful Kamakura Shogunate and the traditional authority of the Imperial Court set the stage for conflict.", "Emperor Go-Toba's fervent patronage of waka poetry fostered a vibrant literary environment in which his son, Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō, could develop his artistic talents.", "The Jōkyū War of 1221, initiated by Emperor Go-Toba to restore imperial power, directly led to the exile and political marginalization of the imperial family, including Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-17T08:08:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27674719244", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Founded through Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Wrote through Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Established through Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Transformed through Dōjo-nyūdōshinnō's historical significance", "type": "Person"}], "newValue": ["Dōjo-